        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<BR>
On Earth, information from the vestibular system is combined with information from gravity sensors in the joints, touch sensors in the skin, and visual information to coordinate body movements and maintain body stability.  However, when space flight crews first experience weightlessness, there is an initial awkwardness in moving within the spacecraft.  There is also disorientation because the signals coming from the vestibular system no longer correspond with visual and other sensory information sent to the brain.  <P>

The Frames of Reference and Internal Models investigation was designed to enhance the understanding of how the central nervous system (CNS) interprets and integrates sensory data for the production of a coordinated motor response.  During the act of catching a ball on Earth, the CNS estimates the trajectory of the ball, accurately taking into account its downward acceleration due to gravity.  This could involve a real-time estimation of ball acceleration based on visual information, but more likely involves the use of <I>a priori </I>information about the effects of gravity on free-falling objects.  This in turn may involve the integration of sensory cues about the actual gravitational environment.  The purpose of this investigation was to test the following hypotheses:<br>
1. If subjects could measure the ball's acceleration, they would correctly estimate time-to-contact (TTC) in both 0-G and 1-G.  <br>
2. A first-order estimate, which assumes no acceleration, predicts TTC correctly in 0-G but overestimates the drop duration at 1-G.<br>
3. If the brain instead uses a second-order internal model to account for gravity's accelerating effects on the ball, motor activity will be correctly timed to impact at 1-G, but the same second-order internal model applied in 0-G will underestimate the true TTC. <br>
<P>
The scientific objective of this experiment involved a ball catching activity in which the CNS was tested to see how it adjusts for the lack of gravitational acceleration acting on the ball in weightlessness.  
 <P>
APPROACH:<BR>
Subjects were seated in a chair and restrained by the body restraint system (BRS).  To complete the scientific objective, subjects were required to catch a ball that was projected "downward" toward the hand by a ball launcher.  Muscle activity in the arm generated in anticipation of contact between the hand and the ball was measured by electromyography (EMG).  Subjects wore the Kinelite headset to verify the correct position of the EMG electrodes before the session.  The Kinelite headset was also used with the Kinelite Noise Generator to generate a tone to mask sounds the ball launcher made.  This tone prevented the subject from using audio cues to predict when the ball would launch.  Movement of the arm was measured by the Camera Illuminator Equipment (CAMILLE), and by accelerometers attached to the limb.  Subjects performed 30 trials of one of three protocols.  For the primary experiment protocol, random, the initial ball velocity varied randomly from trial to trial.  Under these conditions, the subject adjusted motor response "on-line" on the basis of incoming visual information.  For the second protocol, blocked, the initial ball velocity was constant within a given block.  This tested the subject's ability to predict the ball's trajectory given prior knowledge about the initial speed.  The third protocol involved repeating the experiment at a reduced height, thus reducing the viewing time below the minimum required to accurately estimate the time-to-contact on earth.  
<P>
The Kinelite processing module (PM), a computerized video system, recorded and analyzed subject movements and controlled experiment performance.  Then the data was transferred to the Life Sciences Laboratory equipment (LSLE) microcomputer II and subsequently transferred to the ground.  Data for this investigation was collected on L-90, L-60, L-30, FD 3, FD 9, FD 15, R+0, R+1, R+5, R+9, and R+15.
<P>
RESULTS:<BR>
When these experiments were performed on the ground prior to flight, the astronaut subjects generated the peak of anticipatory biceps activity, that occurs about 40 ms prior to impact and independent of initial ball speed, as has been seen previously in other Earth-based studies.  Subjects also rotated their forearm just prior to contact so as to move the hand upward to meet the ball.  This upward movement was <I>not</I> generated by the peak of muscle activity described above, because the movement was initiated somewhat earlier than the measured EMG activity.  Rather, this measurement represented two separate responses – upward movement to meet the ball and limb stiffening to stiffen the hand – both of which can tell us when the brain expects the ball to arrive.  To see whether the brain uses an internal model of gravity when estimating the ball's arrival time, researchers compared the time course of these two responses between 1-G (pre- and postflight) and 0-G (inflight) conditions.  
<P>
Anticipatory muscle activity occurred earlier relative to impact in 0-G compared to 1-G.  This difference could not be explained by the repeated practice on this task that the astronauts had leading up to the flight because the timing returned to preflight values upon return to Earth.  Nor can the shift simply be explained by the fact that subjects had more time to react in 0-G than in 1-G; dropping the ball from higher heights to increase the drop duration on Earth led to no differences in timing.  One might also guess that muscle activity must start sooner during flight to overcome the fact that the muscles tend to be more relaxed in 0-G; but researchers found that supporting the forearm with an externally applied force to counteract gravity on Earth reduced the tonic activation of the biceps (as would be expected in 0-G) while causing no significant changes in timing of the anticipatory EMG peak.  Thus, the timing shifts observed during flight were best explained by a failure to fully compensate for the lack of ball acceleration in 0-G.  
<P>
The timing of forearm movements also changed during flight.  On the ground, such movements were monotonically upward and accurately synchronized to impact, but these movements started much too early in 0-G.  Unlike the simple shift observed for muscle activity onset, however, limb movements stopped or even reversed direction after the initial false start.  The change from monotonic movement on Earth to the non-monotonic movements observed during flight showed that movements were not simply slower in microgravity, as was observed, or simply due to reduced muscle tone in 0-G.  Instead, the evidence suggested that, once triggered, the CNS could correct the ongoing movement by updating estimates of TTC based on visual feedback.
<P>
While one might predict that catching responses would occur too early when subjects are first faced with microgravity conditions, it would be surprising if the brain was unable to adapt to this novel environment.  Some evidence for adaptation exists.  Although forearm movements started prematurely for all inflight tests, on flight days 9 and 15 the amplitude of the early, erroneous movement diminished and a later upward rotation developed just prior to impact.  On the other hand, re-adaptation to Earth conditions occurred almost immediately.  Average data from early postflight sessions (R+0, 1, 2) did not differ significantly from preflight or later postflight sessions.  This indicated that subjects could rapidly retune internal models to the 1-G environment. 
<P>
The key test in this experiment involved determining the magnitude of time shifts for different initial ball velocities (v<sub>0</sub>).  The second-order internal model hypothesis predicted the nonlinear increases in time shifts (d) for decreasing values of v<sub>0</sub> using reasonable, fixed values of the TTC threshold (?).  These ? values were biologically plausible.  Allowing for the time it takes a neural signal to travel from the brain to the periphery, threshold values of TTC used to initiate a given behavior should be somewhat greater than the lead time of the behavior itself.  That is, if the brain wants to activate a muscle <I>x</I> ms prior to ball impact, it has to use a TTC threshold of <I>x+y</I> ms, where <I>y</I> represents the time it takes for the message to get from the brain to the muscles.  
<P>
A response initiated at ?=75 ms within the brain was consistent with the arrival of the EMG peak in the muscle 40 ms prior to impact, due to neural transmission delays.  Similarly, ? values of 300-400 ms were compatible with movement onset that normally occurred 200 ms prior to impact, given the time necessary to overcome limb inertia.  In contrast, a first-order TTC estimate would require unreasonably long ? values (? <U>></U> 1.2 seconds; i.e., longer than the total drop time of the ball in 1-G) to reproduce the time shifts observed in 0-G.  Furthermore, a first-order TTC estimate cannot, with a fixed ?, predict the substantial changes of initiation time as a function of v<sub>0</sub>.  Finally, had the subjects been able to directly measure the acceleration of the ball in real time, one would expect to see no time shift at all (with respect to impact) between 0-G and 1-G, irrespective of ?.
<P>
The data from these experiments were compatible with the third hypothesis; i.e., that to catch a falling ball the CNS: (1) employs a second-order estimate of TTC, (2) uses an <I>internal model</I> to predict gravity's effect on the ball, and (3) applies this internal model initially even in microgravity.  Furthermore, it appeared that the brain gradually adapts the internal model with prolonged exposure to 0-G.  <END>
